and re-ranked, never withheld. This session writes Wave 1's six rows and states that Waves 2, 3
and 4 get theirs "at the boundary each one starts from", so `todos/active/` holds six rows
where the plan holds twenty-three.

Both numbers are in the excerpt and the session printed them itself: `ls todos/active/ | wc -l`
returns `6`, and the shard count read straight out of the plan, `rg -c "^- " …/03-shards.md`,
returns `23`. Seventeen rows of forest exist in the plan and in no todo file. The stated reason
— "writing twenty-three rows today means amending seventeen of them three times" — is the
amendment obligation named as a cost of writing them, which is the disposition the clause
blocks; amendment at each gate is the rule's own answer for later-wave rows, not an argument
against carrying them.

